Ladybird serves ladybird-extension:// URLs by asking the UI process
for the file, decides access once where fetch hands off to the
loader, and answers with the extension’s content security policy,
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, and a cross-origin resource policy.
WebKit’s WebExtensionURLSchemeHandler runs in the UI process: it
refuses a request from a non-extension frame unless
WebExtension::isWebAccessibleResource(requestURL, frameDocumentURL)
says yes, then responds with the bundle’s data, the MIME type from
resourceMIMETypeForPath(), the extension’s contentSecurityPolicy(),
and Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * (WebExtensionURLSchemeHandlerCocoa
.mm). Blink’s ExtensionURLLoaderFactory in the browser process gates
each load with AllowExtensionResourceLoad() (extension_protocols.cc),
which admits the extension’s own process, browser-initiated
navigations, and web-accessible resources through
AllowCrossRendererResourceLoad(), and BuildHttpHeaders() adds the
CSP, COOP/COEP, Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * and
Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin; GenerateBackgroundPage
Contents() synthesizes the background page. Gecko’s
ExtensionProtocolHandler::SubstituteChannel() resolves the file in
the parent, AllowExternalResource() refuses a file outside the
extension directory, and nsContentSecurityManager consults
WebExtensionPolicy::SourceMayAccessPath() for a page’s load of a
moz-extension URL. Treating the scheme as a fetch scheme so
navigations reach the loader is Ladybird-specific plumbing; the
others register the scheme with their URL layers instead.

The UI process checks every extension resource request against its own
registry and against the documents the requesting process hosts. Blink’s
AllowExtensionResourceLoad() checks enablement, process ownership,
and web accessibility in the browser process too, and WebKit’s
WebExtensionURLSchemeHandler decides by the frame’s document URL. Blink
can tell which frame asked, though, and Ladybird can’t yet.

For an about:blank or about:srcdoc document, Ladybird uses the URL of
its nearest same-origin parent or opener, and so does Blink, in
ContentScriptInjectionUrlGetter::Get(), which walks same-origin
ancestors with a cycle guard. Gecko’s MozDocumentMatcher uses DocInfo::
PrincipalURL(), derived from the document’s principal, and WebKit’s
LocalFrame::injectUserScript() uses the parent document’s URL.
None of them ever uses a base URL.
